\title{General Topology}
\date{2026-07-19}
\taxon{Topology}
\import{macros}
\p{
    General topology studies the structures that remain after distance, angle,
    and coordinates have been forgotten. What remains is a rule for deciding
    which subsets are open. From this rule one can recover a useful notion of
    nearness, formulate continuity without a metric, and compare spaces up to
    homeomorphism. The resulting language is broad enough to treat Euclidean
    spaces, function spaces, quotient constructions, and spaces that cannot be
    described by distances at all.
}
\p{
    This note follows the order and conventions of the general-topology part of
    Munkres's [Topology](topology-2000), with occasional comparison to the
    [Stacks Project topology chapter](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004C).
    In particular, compactness means the finite-subcover property and does not
    include Hausdorffness.
}

\subtree{
    \title{Open sets, neighborhoods, and bases}
    \p{
        A topology does not assign a numerical distance to two points. Instead,
        it records which collections of points may serve as observable regions.
        The three topology axioms say that these regions include the trivial
        ones and are stable under the unions and finite intersections needed to
        combine observations.
    }
    \transclude{def-000C}
    \transclude{def-005X}
    \p{
        Open sets describe the space globally. Near one fixed point it is more
        economical to work with neighborhoods, and for the entire topology it
        is often enough to specify a basis. These two reductions will later
        control continuity, countability, and product constructions.
    }
    \transclude{def-005L}
    \transclude{def-005M}
    \p{
        A single set can carry many topologies. At the two extremes, the
        discrete topology declares every subset open, whereas the indiscrete
        topology retains only the opens forced by the axioms. Euclidean
        topology and, on an infinite set, cofinite topology provide less
        degenerate examples between these extremes.
    }
    \transclude{def-005W}
    \transclude{def-000B}
    \transclude{def-000E}
}

\subtree{
    \title{Continuous maps and homeomorphisms}
    \p{
        Once spaces have been specified, the appropriate maps between them are
        those that respect open sets. The preimage formulation is deliberately
        asymmetric: it works for every function, preserves unions and
        intersections, and therefore composes without additional hypotheses.
    }
    \transclude{def-005N}
    \transclude{thm-001C}
    \transclude{thm-001D}
    \p{
        Continuity belongs to a map together with its domain and codomain
        topologies; it is not merely a property of the underlying formula. The
        identity between two differently topologized copies of the same set is
        the smallest example where this dependence becomes visible.
    }
    \transclude{eg-0008}
    \p{
        A homeomorphism is the topological analogue of an isomorphism. It
        permits information to move continuously in both directions, so every
        property expressed solely in terms of the topology is preserved.
    }
    \transclude{def-005O}
    \transclude{eg-000B}
}

\subtree{
    \title{Subspaces, products, and quotients}
    \p{
        Three constructions account for a large fraction of naturally
        occurring spaces. A subset inherits opens from an ambient space, a
        product is observed through its coordinate projections, and a quotient
        is obtained by identifying points. Their definitions are best read
        together using the [comparison of topologies](def-005X): subspace and
        product topologies are the coarsest topologies making the relevant maps
        continuous, while the quotient topology is the finest one detected by
        the quotient map.
    }

    \subtree{
        \title{Subspaces}
        \p{
            Passing to a subspace changes the ambient meaning of openness. An
            endpoint can acquire an open one-sided neighborhood even though no
            such set is open in the real line itself.
        }
        \transclude{def-000D}
        \transclude{thm-001E}
        \transclude{eg-0009}
    }

    \subtree{
        \title{Products}
        \p{
            The product topology permits observations in finitely many
            coordinates at a time. This finite-coordinate condition is
            invisible for ordinary finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces but is
            essential for infinite products.
        }
        \transclude{def-005V}
        \transclude{thm-001G}
        \transclude{eg-000D}
    }

    \subtree{
        \title{Quotients}
        \p{
            A quotient reverses the direction of construction. Instead of
            restricting an existing topology, it asks which subsets of the
            target can be recognized as open after pulling them back to the
            source. Its universal property is the practical way to construct
            maps out of a quotient.
        }
        \transclude{def-005P}
        \transclude{thm-001F}
    }
}

\subtree{
    \title{Connectedness}
    \p{
        Connectedness asks whether a space can be decomposed into two open
        pieces without overlap. This is a global property: every sufficiently
        small portion of a space may look simple while the whole space still
        splits into disconnected pieces.
    }
    \transclude{def-005Q}
    \p{
        The least-upper-bound property of the real numbers prevents an interval
        from splitting at an omitted boundary point. This supplies the basic
        source of connected examples in analysis.
    }
    \transclude{thm-001O}
    \p{
        The next theorem makes connectedness useful under constructions. A
        continuous map may collapse points, but it cannot tear one connected
        space into a disconnected image.
    }
    \transclude{thm-001H}
}

\subtree{
    \title{Compactness and Hausdorff separation}
    \p{
        Compactness replaces an arbitrary amount of local covering data by a
        finite amount. It is a topological finiteness condition, not a statement
        about numerical boundedness. Closed bounded intervals motivate the
        definition, but the definition continues to make sense in every
        topological space.
    }
    \transclude{def-005R}
    \transclude{thm-001P}
    \transclude{thm-001I}
    \transclude{thm-001J}
    \p{
        Hausdorffness and compactness do not imply one another. Hausdorffness
        requires distinct points to admit disjoint observations and thereby
        rules out several pathologies of coarse topologies. It passes to
        subspaces and products, though an arbitrary quotient of a Hausdorff
        space need not remain Hausdorff.
    }
    \transclude{def-005S}
    \transclude{thm-001N}
    \transclude{thm-001K}
    \p{
        The Euclidean real line is Hausdorff but noncompact. In the other
        direction, an infinite cofinite space is compact and connected, yet it
        is not Hausdorff.
    }
    \transclude{eg-000H}
    \transclude{eg-000E}
    \p{
        Compactness and Hausdorffness become especially effective when they
        occur on opposite sides of a continuous bijection. Compactness makes
        closed information survive the map, and Hausdorffness recognizes the
        image as closed. This supplies a widely used criterion for proving that
        a bijection is a homeomorphism.
    }
    \transclude{thm-001L}
    \p{
        We can now return to the quotient construction. Gluing the two
        endpoints of a compact interval produces a circle, and the criterion
        above turns the evident continuous bijection into a homeomorphism.
    }
    \transclude{eg-000A}
}

\subtree{
    \title{Countability axioms}
    \p{
        A basis may still be too large for explicit arguments. The two standard
        countability axioms ask whether local neighborhoods or the entire
        topology can be controlled by countable data. The second condition is
        stronger because one global basis must work simultaneously at every
        point.
    }
    \transclude{def-005T}
    \transclude{thm-001M}
    \p{
        Rational endpoints give a countable basis for the Euclidean real line.
        In contrast, an uncountable discrete space has a one-element local
        basis at every point but cannot have a countable global basis.
    }
    \transclude{eg-000G}
    \transclude{eg-000C}
    \p{
        First-countability explains why sequences suffice for many arguments in
        metric spaces. Without such a hypothesis, sequences alone may fail to
        detect closure or continuity; nets or filters are then the appropriate
        general substitutes.
    }
}

\subtree{
    \title{Local compactness}
    \p{
        A noncompact space may nevertheless look compact around each point.
        Local compactness records this intermediate situation. It is strong
        enough to support many constructions from analysis while still
        including open subsets of Euclidean spaces and manifolds.
    }
    \transclude{def-005U}
    \transclude{eg-000F}
    \p{
        Keeping Hausdorffness separate from local compactness makes the
        convention explicit. It also prevents equivalent formulations from the
        Hausdorff setting from being used automatically in spaces where those
        formulations may diverge.
    }
}

\p{
    The concepts above form the basic toolkit of point-set topology. Bases
    present topologies, continuous maps compare them, subspaces, products, and
    quotients construct new spaces, and connectedness, compactness,
    Hausdorffness, countability, and local compactness measure the resulting
    spaces. Natural next steps include stronger separation axioms, the
    Tychonoff theorem, metrization theorems, and compactifications.
}
